menu "Power Base"

    config POWER_BASE_BENCH_JOINER_CONSOLE
        bool "Bench-only: accept a Joiner PSKd on the USB-Serial/JTAG console"
        depends on OPENTHREAD_JOINER && ESP_CONSOLE_USB_SERIAL_JTAG
        default n
        help
            Development/bench trial path for pb_thread:mode=2 (AWAIT_COMMISSIONING).
            Adds one console command, "pb_join", that reads a one-time PSKd without
            echo and runs the OpenThread Joiner. It does not enable the OpenThread
            CLI. Leave this off in every image flashed to an operating Power Base;
            it is set only by power_base/sdkconfig.bench_joiner (see
            docs/power-base.md). The BLE transport is the separate
            POWER_BASE_BLE_ENROLLMENT option below; the two are never built
            together.

    config POWER_BASE_BLE_ENROLLMENT
        bool "BLE enrollment transport for pb_thread:mode=2 (protocomm Security 2)"
        depends on OPENTHREAD_JOINER && BT_NIMBLE_ENABLED
        depends on ESP_PROTOCOMM_SUPPORT_SECURITY_VERSION_2
        depends on !ESP_PROTOCOMM_SUPPORT_SECURITY_VERSION_0 && !ESP_PROTOCOMM_SUPPORT_SECURITY_VERSION_1
        depends on !POWER_BASE_BENCH_JOINER_CONSOLE && !OPENTHREAD_CLI
        default n
        help
            Trial build for a spare Power Base (pb_thread:mode=2): while the board
            is not enrolled it advertises a BLE service through which a client
            that knows this board's setup code (protocomm Security 2, SRP-6a +
            AES-GCM) can hand over a one-time Thread PSKd and start the same
            Joiner enrollment the bench console starts. Needs a "pb_ble_auth"
            credential in the cfg partition (tools/gen_power_base_ble_config.py);
            without a valid one BLE stays off and the board just waits.
            Set only by power_base/sdkconfig.ble_enrollment, with its own build
            directory and SDKCONFIG file (see docs/power-base.md). It contains no
            pb_join console and no OpenThread CLI.

    config POWER_BASE_REENROLL_BUTTON
        bool "Local re-enrollment by a long press of a button (pb_thread:mode=2, BLE build only)"
        depends on POWER_BASE_BLE_ENROLLMENT
        default n
        help
            Lets the owner of an ENROLLED pb_thread:mode=2 Power Base move it to
            another hub without a computer and without its old hub: holding the
            button for 5 s and releasing it restarts the board into a 120 s
            "re-enrollment window", in which Thread and every service stay off
            and only the BLE service runs. A client that authenticates with the
            board's own setup code (Security 2) and sends an explicitly
            confirmed local reset command makes the board erase its own
            enrollment record and stored Thread network and verify that. The
            button alone, a BLE connection alone and a successful
            authentication alone erase nothing; a window that ends without
            such a command restarts into the ordinary boot, which resumes the
            enrollment as it was. See docs/power-base.md "BOOT 버튼 로컬 재등록".

            Off in the default, bench and plain BLE builds: those behave as
            before. It is never available in pb_thread modes 0/1, in the module
            firmware or in the RCP. The button GPIO is checked at boot against
            the pins the board's cfg already uses (POGO UART, DETECT, power
            enable) and against the chip's reserved pins; on a conflict the
            feature stays off for that boot.

    config POWER_BASE_REENROLL_BUTTON_GPIO
        int "GPIO of the re-enrollment button"
        depends on POWER_BASE_REENROLL_BUTTON
        range 0 27
        default 9
        help
            GPIO9 is the ESP32-H2 BOOT strapping pin, which development boards
            usually wire to a "BOOT" push button to ground. The ROM samples it
            at reset only (held low at reset = download mode); this feature
            reads it as an ordinary input after boot and never asks for it to
            be held at power-on or reset. Whether a given board really has a
            button on this pin is a HARDWARE ASSUMPTION that must be checked on
            that board -- see docs/power-base.md.

    config POWER_BASE_REENROLL_BUTTON_ACTIVE_LOW
        bool "The button pulls its GPIO low when pressed"
        depends on POWER_BASE_REENROLL_BUTTON
        default y
        help
            y: pressed = low, the internal pull-up is enabled (a button to
            ground, like the usual BOOT button). n: pressed = high, the
            internal pull-down is enabled.

endmenu
